#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Answer Unio Bot's inbox: mentions and follow-ups in task and project comments, assignments.

    unio-inbox                       poll every UNIO_INBOX_INTERVAL seconds (the service)
    unio-inbox --once                one poll: claim, react, answer, then exit
    unio-inbox --dry-run             list the pending items without claiming any
    unio-inbox --reviews --dry-run   list the project review candidates and which would run
    unio-inbox --needs --dry-run     list the tasks waiting on a person and what a check would do
    unio-inbox --workspace org_<id> ...    any of the above with that workspace's profile
    unio-inbox --workspace org_<id> --print-paths    the profile's folders and token file, as JSON
    unio-inbox --workspace org_<id> --check          whether the profile can answer, as JSON (exit 0 or 1)

Each item is claimed on the server (so no machine or restart handles it
twice), marked with an eyes reaction, and answered by one headless Claude Code
session that has the Unio tools, scoped on the server to the item's live claim,
its team and what its author may do, and read-only snapshots of the team's
repositories. The session follows the responder protocol (see Files) and ends
with a JSON outcome line, which is checked against the tool calls it made. The
item is then completed; failed, so the server retries it (three attempts in
all); or released without spending an attempt when the machine is the problem.
Low memory is one, on Linux: under 4 GiB available, under 1 GiB of free swap
with under 8 GiB available, or a critical reading from unio-desk (its
desk.json, under 90 s old) holds answers for 5 minutes and skips the reviews
then due. A profile instead holds only under UNIO_INBOX_MEMORY_FLOOR_GIB
available, when set, or on a critical desk.json.

Every prompt names the agent's writing language (GET /agents/me), and what the
runner posts itself comes in that language, English or Brazilian Portuguese.
A responder protocol holding the workspace marker gets the agent's teams and
their repositories written in for each run.

The runner tells Unio what it is doing (idle, the item and step it works on,
or the problem that holds it), so the workspace sees it on the agent's page.

Project reviews, with UNIO_REVIEW=on: inside a daily look window,
when a poll found no item, the runner asks Unio which projects changed since
their latest update and runs one session per chosen project, between inbox
polls. That session has only get_project and post_project_update, scoped on
the server to the project (X-Unio-Review-Project), follows
the review protocol, and most often posts nothing. UNIO_REVIEW=observe
logs what would run and runs nothing; off (the default) never asks.

Needs checks, with UNIO_NEEDS=on: when a poll found no item and ran no review,
at most every UNIO_NEEDS_INTERVAL, the runner finds the tasks whose description
holds a "## Needs <first name>" task list (a search per person the agent can
see, plus the tasks it remembers) and keeps the "Needs action" label in step
with it: on while an item is open; once none is, off, with the ticked sections
(a section is its heading and the task list right under it, nothing more).
For one task a pass whose facts changed (its update time, its comments, the
GitHub state of the pull requests it links in repos.json's repositories), or
that it judged UNIO_NEEDS_RECHECK ago, a session with only get_task and
list_comments on that task follows the needs protocol and names the items
done; the runner ticks them. Its writes go through the MCP endpoint, each
against the task's updatedAt as read. UNIO_NEEDS=observe logs what it would do
and writes nothing; off (the default) never looks.

Profiles: with --workspace org_<id>, everything belongs to that workspace's
profile, one per workspace on a computer (unio-runner sets them up):
  <config>/unio/runners/<org>/    runner-token, repos.json and runner-id, as below;
                                  env, KEY=VALUE lines read at start (the real
                                  environment wins); and responder.md, reviewer.md
                                  or needs.md to replace the protocol of that name
                                  in protocol/ beside this file
  <state>/unio/runners/<org>/     what ~/.local/state/unio/inbox/ holds below
<config> is $XDG_CONFIG_HOME or ~/.config and <state> is $XDG_STATE_HOME or
~/.local/state on Linux and macOS; on Windows they are %APPDATA% and %LOCALAPPDATA%.
A profile's service keeps itself current: this file lives in a clone of its
repository, and at start and every 6 h, between polls, the runner fetches main;
when main's runner/VERSION is higher and the clone is a clean main, it
fast-forwards and exits with status 75 for the service manager to start the new
code. UNIO_RUNNER_AUTOUPDATE=off turns that off.
Without --workspace, the runner keeps its single-machine files:

Files:
  ~/.config/unio/token            the agent token (unio-auth)
  ~/.config/unio/responder.md     the responder protocol
  ~/.config/unio/reviewer.md      the review protocol; without it reviews are off
  ~/.config/unio/needs.md         the needs check protocol; without it no check runs
  ~/.config/unio/repos.json       team identifier -> GitHub repositories (owner/name)
  ~/.config/unio/runner-id        this install's presence id (written on first use)
  ~/.local/state/unio/inbox/      health.json, log.jsonl, reviews.json, needs.json,
                                  runs/<item>/, runs/review-<project>/, runs/needs-<task>/,
                                  repos/<owner>__<name>/

Environment: UNIO_API_URL (default https://api.unio.so/v1),
UNIO_INBOX_INTERVAL (30 s), UNIO_INBOX_TIMEOUT (600 s), UNIO_INBOX_MAX_TURNS (30),
UNIO_INBOX_MAX_BUDGET_USD (1, per session), UNIO_INBOX_DAILY_BUDGET_USD (15, over
any 24 h), UNIO_INBOX_HOURLY_SESSIONS (20), UNIO_INBOX_MODEL, UNIO_INBOX_CLAUDE
and UNIO_INBOX_GH (the executables; default: claude and gh on PATH).
UNIO_INBOX_WEB (off, search or on; default off): search lets an inbox run use web
search, on also lets it read the pages it finds.
UNIO_RUNNER_AUTOUPDATE (on or off; default on): a profile's self-update.
UNIO_INBOX_MEMORY_FLOOR_GIB (a profile on Linux; default none): GiB of memory
available below which answers wait.
UNIO_REVIEW (off, observe or on; default off), UNIO_REVIEW_WINDOW (06:30-09:00),
UNIO_REVIEW_TZ (America/Sao_Paulo) and UNIO_REVIEW_MODEL (default: the inbox's).
UNIO_NEEDS (off, observe or on; default off), UNIO_NEEDS_INTERVAL (15 min between
passes), UNIO_NEEDS_RECHECK (6 h before an unchanged task is judged again; the
wait doubles after each check that finds nothing, up to 8 times, and a done or
canceled task is never rechecked), UNIO_NEEDS_DAILY_RUNS (30 checks over any
24 h, rechecks at most half of them) and UNIO_NEEDS_MODEL (default: the
inbox's). Checks count toward UNIO_INBOX_DAILY_BUDGET_USD, as reviews do.
"""
